9
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,240
for killing the Jews of Europe and approximately one million people,  

10
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:08,200
probably about 90 percent of the victims of the Auschwitz concentration camp,  

11
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:14,040
would die there. Among the Nazi staff at the camp is a German doctor who,  

12
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:19,160
As a member of the SS, he participated in the selections for the gas chambers and  

13
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:26,000
He conducts experiments on prisoners in the camps. His name is Horst Fischer.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,600
Horst Paul Silvester Fischer was born on December 31, 1912 in Dresden,  

15
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:38,440
then part of the German Empire. After the death of his parents, he grew up an orphan  

16
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:43,960
and was raised by his uncle in Berlin. Under his uncle's care, he was instilled with the  

17
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:50,320
völkisch values - these were nationalist, emphasizing a sense of ethnic purity and  

18
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:56,880
a strong national identity. These early influences would shape their worldview and  

19
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:02,600
They would later play an important role in his political and ideological beliefs.

20
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:07,880
In his youth, Fischer became involved with the Bündische Jugend, a movement  

21
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:15,240
German youth movement that emerged in the 1920s. The movement promoted outdoor activities,  

22
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:21,600
self-discipline and a sense of community, focusing on returning to nature and  

23
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:27,760
fostering independence among young people. These ideals appealed to Fischer and reinforced  

24
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:33,360
their growing sense of nationalism and their commitment to traditional values. 

25
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:39,720
In 1932, Fischer began studying medicine at the University of Berlin,  

26
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:45,040
completing his studies with the state exam five years later.

27
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:51,440
On January 30, 1933, Adolf Hitler was appointed Chancellor of Germany by the  

28
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,040
President Paul von Hindenburg. In November of the same year,  

29
00:02:55,040 --> 00:03:03,960
Fischer became a member of the SS and in May 1937 he also joined the Nazi Party.

30
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:08,960
World War II began on September 1, 1939,  

31
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:13,160
when Germany invaded Poland. The early stages of the war saw  

32
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:18,960
rapid German advances in Europe, with the fall of Poland, the invasion of France and the evacuation  

33
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:26,440
of the British forces at Dunkirk in 1940. After the outbreak of war, Fischer  

34
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:31,560
He initially served in various roles as a medical officer in the Waffen-SS,  

35
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:37,440
the military branch of the SS, participating in both military operations and in concentration camps  

36
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:45,280
concentration camps like Dachau and Sachsenhausen. With all this, on Sunday, June 22, 1941,  

37
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:51,120
Under the code name Operation Barbarossa, Nazi Germany invaded the Soviet Union.

38
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:56,840
Fischer participated in the invasion of the Soviet Union, but after contracting tuberculosis,  

39
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:01,720
He was removed from front-line service. During his recovery in a sanatorium,  

40
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:07,920
Fischer met Enno Lolling, the head of Office D III of the Main Economic Office  

41
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:14,120
and Administrative of the SS. This was responsible for supervising medical services and hygiene  

42
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:19,000
in the concentration camps. Lolling, impressed by the medical records  

43
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,360
Fischer offered him a position in a concentration camp to improve his skills  

44
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:30,960
surgical. Fischer accepted and, a few months later, received a draft order to serve  

45
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:36,280
as a field doctor at Auschwitz-Birkenau, located in German-occupied Poland.

46
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:43,280
Birkenau was the largest of the more than 40 camps and subcamps that made up the Auschwitz complex.  

47
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:48,360
It was divided into ten sections, separated by electrified barbed wire fences,  

48
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:57,040
and patrolled by SS guards, including, from 1942, SS dog handlers. 

49
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:03,320
During its three years of operation, Birkenau served multiple functions. When the  

50
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:11,040
Construction in October 1941, it was initially planned to be a camp for 125,000 prisoners  

51
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:18,080
of war. It opened as a branch of Auschwitz in March 1942 and, at the same time,  

52
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:25,560
It functioned as a center for the extermination of Jews. In its final phase, from 1944 onwards,  

53
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:31,080
It also became a detention area where prisoners were concentrated before  

54
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:37,120
from being transferred to forced labor in German industry throughout the Third Reich. 

55
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:42,480
As part of their duties, the medical staff at Auschwitz made the calls  

56
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:48,840
"selections". The objective of these selections was to identify people who could not  

57
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:55,240
working, which the SS considered "useless mouths to feed" and which later  

58
00:05:55,240 --> 00:06:01,400
were murdered. When the transports of Jews arrived at Birkenau, the camp's medical staff  

59
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:07,600
He selected healthy adults to perform forced labor. Those who were not selected,  

60
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:12,720
including children and the elderly, were sent to the gas chambers to be murdered.

61
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:21,280
Fischer arrived at Auschwitz-Birkenau on November 6, 1942. From November 1943  

62
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:27,880
Until September 1944, he worked as chief physician at the Monowitz concentration camp,  

63
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:33,760
a subcamp of Auschwitz, where prisoners were forced to do labor for the company  

64
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:40,560
IG Farben chemical company. This company played a pivotal role in the Nazi war effort,  

65
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:45,840
as it produced synthetic rubber and fuel, and also supplied Zyklon B,  

66
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:51,840
the gas used in the extermination chambers, which killed more than a million people at Auschwitz. 

67
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:57,600
In Monowitz, the working conditions of forced laborers were brutal.  

68
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:03,000
Due to inhumane working conditions, the maximum life expectancy of workers  

69
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:09,560
was only six months old. During his stay at Auschwitz, Fischer played a direct role in the  

70
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:14,440
death of tens of thousands of prisoners, as he carried out selections on the ramp,  

71
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:18,720
in the prison hospital, in the roll call area and in the barracks,  

72
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:23,040
as well as in the work units. In addition, Fischer was responsible for  

73
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:30,640
approve flogging sentences of between 5 and 25 lashes in 71 cases and, in 51 cases,  

74
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:35,800
He personally witnessed the execution of the punishments. He also participated in experiments  

75
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:41,880
with electroshock devices, supposedly to boost the morale of depressed prisoners.

76
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:48,760
From April 1943, Fischer worked regularly on the selection ramp and decided  

77
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:53,600
who would be sent to forced labor and who would go directly to the chambers  

78
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:59,800
of gas. Their selections often targeted the most vulnerable, including mothers  

79
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:04,160
with children, the elderly and the sick. When asked after the  

80
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:09,960
war if it sent these groups to their deaths, Fischer responded chillingly:  

81
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:14,960
"If I were on duty, I'd send them all to be gassed."  

82
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:21,280
He even admitted to timing the deaths in the gas chambers through a peephole.

83
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:25,440
In total, it is estimated that he was directly responsible for sending people to their deaths in the  

84
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:29,920
gas chambers to at least 70,000 prisoners. The horrific  

85
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:36,080
level of involvement weighed heavily on Fischer, and during his time in the camp, he reportedly  

86
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:41,720
He commented things like, "We've come so far that we can't go back." 

87
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:48,200
In June 1944, Fischer was promoted to Hauptsturmführer, the equivalent of the rank  

88
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:54,960
of captain, making him one of the highest-ranking SS doctors at Auschwitz.

89
00:08:54,960 --> 00:09:00,440
World War II in Europe officially ended on May 8, 1945.  

90
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,800
